<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexander II of Russia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1818">1818</a>–<a href="/years/1881">1881</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Alexander II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alexander II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> was emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> from 1855 to 1881, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> Liberator. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-reforms-russia">Great Reforms</a> included the emancipation of the serfs in 1861, together with changes to local government, the courts and the army. He was assassinated by revolutionaries in 1881.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia.jpg" alt="Alexander II of Russia" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to W. &amp; D. Downey · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zar_Alexander_II_(cropped)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alexander Nikolayevich was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-i-of-russia">Nicholas I of Russia</a> and Charlotte of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. He became Tsesarevich in 1825, when his father took the throne. Under Nicholas the intellectual atmosphere of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> suppressed freedom of thought. The poet Vasily Zhukovsky supervised his education. In 1837 he toured twenty provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and was the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-romanov">Romanov</a> heir to visit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>.</p><p>He met Princess Marie of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a> on a tour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and they married in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> on 16 April 1841. The marriage produced six sons and two daughters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Alexander became emperor on 2 March 1855. On 15 January 1856 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a> on the unfavourable terms of the Treaty of Paris, which cost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea-fleet">Black Sea Fleet</a>. This gave him room for domestic reform: the Edict of Emancipation of 1861, which abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>, and changes to the courts, local self-government, censorship, schools and the army. Many reforms met intense backlash and were cut back, and after an assassination attempt in 1866 he moved towards a more conservative stance.</p><p>In 1863 a Polish uprising led him to strip <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> of its separate constitution and incorporate the kingdom directly into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. In 1867 he sold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1877–1878 brought independence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. He also approved military plans in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> that culminated in the Circassian genocide.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After a last attempt on his life in February 1880, Count Loris-Melikov was given extraordinary powers against the revolutionaries, and his proposals called for a form of parliamentary body. On 13 March 1881 (1 March in the old-style calendar) Alexander was assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>. A bomb thrown by Nikolai Rysakov of the Narodnaya Volya damaged his carriage but left him unhurt, and a second member, Ignacy Hryniewiecki, then threw something at his feet.</p><p>Alexander had approved Loris-Melikov's plans as the first step towards a constitution. His successor, Alexander III, on the advice of Konstantin Pobedonostsev, abandoned them. The assassination brought suppression of civil liberties, use of the Okhrana secret police, and anti-Jewish pogroms and the May Laws.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alexander II of Russia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Empire's social structure was defined above all by serfdom, which bound roughly a third of the total population, and a far higher proportion of the rural workforce, to the land and to the legal authority of noble landowners until Tsar <b>Alexander II</b>'s Emancipation Edict of 19 February 1861.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>On 26 August 1856, on the accession of <b>Alexander II</b>, the Decembrists were amnestied and their rights restored, though many chose to stay in Siberia.</blockquote><a href="/movement/decembrist-revolt">Decembrist revolt · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Russian Empire Catherine II confined Jews to the Pale of Settlement in 1772, and the May Laws of 1882 followed the assassination of <b>Alexander II</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/antisemitism">Antisemitism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>Three emperors ruled in these decades: Alexander I (1801–1825), who drove Napoleon’s army out of Russia in 1812; his brother Nicholas I (1825–1855), who began his reign by crushing the Decembrist revolt of noble officers; and Nicholas’s son <b>Alexander II</b> (1855–1881), who freed the serfs.</blockquote><a href="/societies/russian-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Russian Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Hildebrand of Sovana was elected pope as Gregory VII, succeeding Pope <b>Alexander II</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-22">22 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>At York, Henry III of England and <b>Alexander II</b> of Scotland agreed that Northumberland, Cumberland and Westmorland were subject to English sovereignty.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-25">25 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Small Nicholas Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in Winter Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1858" aria-label="Small Nicholas Palace on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5370 263 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alexander II of Russia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5820 533h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1858">What was here in 1858? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
